  "articleBody": "Seeing fresh paint blister, bubble, or peel away from your bathroom wall just weeks after a remodel is frustrating. In almost every case, premature paint failure comes down to moisture trapped beneath the film or surface contamination that prevented a proper chemical bond. Bathrooms produce intense humidity spikes, and ordinary interior wall paint simply cannot handle warm steam without proper preparation.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice:\n- Surface prep matters: Bathrooms collect hairspray, soap residue, and body oils. Painting over these films guarantees poor adhesion, causing the paint to lift when humidity rises.\n- Moisture trapped behind walls: If a plumbing pipe has a minor leak or exterior water is getting into the wall cavity, moisture pushes through the drywall from behind, blowing off the paint.\n- Sheen and coating type: Flat builder-grade paints absorb moisture easily. High-moisture areas need specialized paints formulated with antimicrobial agents and a satin or semi-gloss finish that sheds droplets.\n- Recoat and cure timing: A wall can feel dry to the touch in two hours, but full water-resistant curing can take days. Turning on a steamy shower before the paint film cures causes the binders to soften and blister.\n\nWhen you notice recurring peeling or soft drywall around your tub, you need a pro to check the moisture levels before repainting. \n\nHH Remodeling LLC handles bathroom drywall repairs and moisture-rated painting across Aurora and the Denver metro area. Call 303-505-0866 to get your bathroom walls finished right.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
